BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Why do queens mate so much? (research hoo
 k)
DTSTART:20260803T150000Z
DTEND:20260803T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-queenmating-20260803@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot\n"Why do quee
 ns mate so much?" — reserved research hook\, pre-verified Jul 22 2026\n\
 nExpert-authorship check: PASSED — all three authors (Tarpy\, Delaney\, 
 Seeley) are in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md. Dr. Tarpy is our confirmed scientif
 ic advisor\; Dr. Delaney was emailed 2026-07-25 (awaiting reply)\; Dr. See
 ley is a lead in the University Research Program Contacts list.\n\nPost te
 xt:\nTurns out one of honey bees' strangest traits might be a survival tri
 ck. Queens mate with 10-20+ drones each — far more than needed just for 
 genetic diversity. A 2015 study by Dr. David Tarpy\, Dr. Debbie Delaney\, 
 and Dr. Thomas Seeley (our own scientific advisor among them) genotyped of
 fspring from feral colonies in NY's Arnot Forest and found their queens ma
 te at the same high rate as managed-colony queens — no more\, no less. T
 hat points to something interesting: this level of polyandry likely evolve
 d over deep evolutionary time\, not as a recent adaptation to survive Varr
 oa. It's baked in. Every non-managed colony we map carries that same trait
  — one more reason each one is worth finding. savethehives.org\n\nImage:
  images/facebook/oak-tree-colony_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (new pick this ru
 n — unused so far\, hollow-tree colony shot fits the "every non-managed 
 colony we map" line\; also available as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\n\nAlt
  text: "A honey bee colony's wax comb visible inside the cavity of a hollo
 w oak tree."\n\nLink: keep the trailing "savethehives.org" as plain text 
 — a photo is attached so no Facebook link-preview card will generate.\n\
 nSource: Tarpy DR\, Delaney DA\, Seeley TD (2015). "Mating Frequencies of 
 Honey Bee Queens (Apis mellifera L.) in a Population of Feral Colonies in 
 the Northeastern United States." PLOS ONE 10(3). https://journals.plos.org
 /plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0118734\n\nSuggested timing: Mon 
 Aug 3\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Not every nest is a honey bee colony (bee
  ID)
DTSTART:20260805T150000Z
DTEND:20260805T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-beeid-20260805@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot\n"Not
  every buzzing nest is a honey bee colony" (bee ID / logging accuracy)\n\n
 Post text:\nNot every buzzing nest you find is a honey bee colony — and 
 that's fine\, it happens to everyone.\n\nHoney bees build vertical wax com
 b inside a cavity (a hollow tree\, wall void\, or bee box)\, and you'll us
 ually see a steady stream of bees flying in and out\, even on cool days. B
 umblebees and yellowjackets nest in small ground colonies or paper nests a
 nd don't look anything like that. If you're not sure what you found\, log 
 it anyway — a photo is enough for us to help sort it out\, and every sub
 mission\, even a maybe\, helps keep the map honest.\n\nsavethehives.org\n\
 nImage: images/facebook/learn-bee-box_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (FB-cropped 
 version of the Learn tab reference asset\; also available as _fb-portrait-
 1080x1350.jpg)\n\nAlt text: "Illustration of a honey bee hive box used as 
 a reference for identifying wild colonies."\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL
  as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n
 \nSuggested timing: Wed Aug 5\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: second ongoing-rotati
 on Wednesday post. New content\, not in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — How this started (origin story)
DTSTART:20260809T150000Z
DTEND:20260809T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-howthisstarted-20260809@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot\n"How th
 is started" (ready post from FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4\, added Jul 22 2
 026)\n\nPost text:\nBefore this was an app\, it was a friendship. SaveTheH
 ives actually started in 2008 — Ronnie was an active beekeeper and met D
 r. David Tarpy at an NC State beekeepers meeting. One thing led to another
 \, and the two started "The Feral Bee Project": researchers needed known l
 ocations of wild honey bee colonies for genetic sampling tied to Colony Co
 llapse Disorder research\, so we built a simple web map for anyone to log 
 what they found. By 2012 there was even an iPhone app (Honey Bee Mapper). 
 The project got cited in outside bee research and grew entries from around
  the world before the original site went dark around 2015.\n\nThose origin
 al 1\,000+ records never disappeared — they're the backbone of the map y
 ou see today\, and Dr. Tarpy is back on board as scientific advisor. Same 
 mission\, 18 years later. savethehives.org\n\nImage: no dedicated asset fi
 ts — consider a fresh or period photo from the original 2008-2012 projec
 t if Ronnie has one. Fallback: images/facebook/map-screenshot-cropped_fb-s
 quare-1080x1080.jpg (repeat risk: already used for Post 3).\n\nAlt text: s
 et based on whichever image is used.\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as pla
 in text if a photo is attached — no preview card will generate.\n\nSourc
 e: full origin story in PROJECT_HISTORY.md (Era 0)\n\nSuggested timing: Su
 n Aug 9\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — CA hybrid bees show natural Varroa resist
 ance (research hook)
DTSTART:20260810T150000Z
DTEND:20260810T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-cahybrid-20260810@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot — first FRE
 SH research-hook search since resuming Aug 10 2026 (per FACEBOOK_POST_LOG.
 md standing note)\n\nExpert-authorship check: no direct name match against
  KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md. FLAGGED for Ronnie: lead/corresponding author Bor
 is Baer (UC Riverside\, Center for Integrative Bee Research/CIBER) is a st
 rong circumstantial match for the "Southern California Africanized bee res
 earch group" already listed as an Unconfirmed Lead in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.
 md — Dr. Tarpy mentioned working with a SoCal group studying Africanized
 /hybrid bees that received a grant that took two years to materialize\; th
 is study's Southern California hybrid population (Western European x Easte
 rn European x Middle Eastern x African lineage) fits that description clos
 ely. Worth confirming with Tarpy directly and adding Baer/CIBER to KEY_PEO
 PLE_CONTACTS.md if confirmed — do not add automatically.\n\nPost text:\n
 New research is validating exactly what SaveTheHives hunts for. A study ou
 t of UC Riverside's Center for Integrative Bee Research tracked 236 honey 
 bee colonies in Southern California over four years and found something st
 riking: a hybrid population that survives without human intervention consi
 stently had lower Varroa mite levels than commercial stock — and in lab 
 tests\, mites were less attracted to their larvae in the first place. Fewe
 r mites\, fewer treatments needed\, no help required.\n\nThat's the whole 
 thesis behind Genetic Goldmines: colonies that persist on their own aren't
  just lucky\, they're carrying something worth studying. Every pin on our 
 map could be a lead like this one. savethehives.org\n\nImage: no asset spe
 cifically depicts this study. Fallback: images/facebook/honeybee-on-comb_f
 b-square-1080x1080.jpg (repeat risk: already used for Post 1) or consider 
 a fresh photo.\n\nAlt text: set based on whichever image is used.\n\nLink:
  keep the trailing URL as plain text if a photo is attached.\n\nSource: Ba
 er\, B. et al. (2026). "Varroa mite resistance in a hybrid honey bee (Apis
  mellifera) population in Southern California." Scientific Reports 16\, 10
 952. https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-45759-9\n\nSuggested timin
 g: Mon Aug 10\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content this run\, not in FACEBOO
 K_STARTER_PACK.md — generated per §5 Monday science/mission slot\, cros
 s-referenced against §6's general "Californian hybrid honeybees" hook wit
 h the actual primary source now identified.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — The whole toolkit fits in a box
DTSTART:20260812T150000Z
DTEND:20260812T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-toolkitbox-20260812@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot\n"The
  whole toolkit fits in a box" (ready post from FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §
 4\, added Jul 28 2026)\n\nPost text:\nThis little wooden box is the entire
  toolkit for beelining. Two compartments\, a scrap of honeycomb\, and a li
 d. Catch a foraging bee\, let her fill up on syrup inside\, then open the 
 lid and watch which way she flies home — dead straight\, every time.\n\n
 That's it. No expensive gear\, no bees to own. Just patience and a compass
 . Full how-to in the Learn tab: savethehives.org/?tab=learn\n\nImage: Ronn
 ie's own photo of a bee-lining box in a wildflower field — saved locally
 \, NOT in the repo (unlike other post images). Needs to be attached manual
 ly from his own files.\n\nAlt text: "A wooden bee-lining box with glass vi
 ewing panels and an engraved honey bee\, sitting on a wooden fence post in
  a wildflower field."\n\nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn as trailing
  plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nS
 uggested timing: Wed Aug 12\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: deliberately a real pho
 to rather than the app's watercolor bee-box illustration (already used Aug
  5) — keeps the two bee-box posts visually distinct.\n\nFull copy in FAC
 EBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — About this project (full-length anchor po
 st)
DTSTART:20260816T150000Z
DTEND:20260816T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260802T220607Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-aboutproject-20260816@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot\n"About 
 this project" (ready post from FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §8\, added Jul 22
  2026)\n\nJUDGMENT CALL flagged for Ronnie: §8 notes this post ideally ru
 ns alongside a "meet our advisor" follow-up once Dr. Tarpy sends his bio s
 ynopsis\, which hasn't arrived yet. No other Sunday-slot content (found-co
 lony spotlight\, milestone post) is ready yet\, so this was used to fill t
 he Aug 16 slot on its own. Fine to hold and swap in something else if Tarp
 y's synopsis or a real submission spotlight comes in before then.\n\nPost 
 text:\nSaveTheHives is a free\, volunteer-run citizen science project mapp
 ing non-managed honey bee colonies across North America. Over 1\,150 legac
 y records dating back to 2008 are already on the map\, and growing.\n\nOur
  focus: colonies that persist winter after winter with zero human treatmen
 t — what we call Genetic Goldmines — because each one is a real candid
 ate for understanding how honey bees might survive without constant chemic
 al support.\n\nAnyone can log a wild hive or confirm one that's already on
  the map\, no experience or equipment required.\n\nScientific framing revi
 ewed and approved by Dr. David Tarpy\, NC State University Scholar Profess
 or & Extension Apiculturist.\n\nsavethehives.org\n\nImage: images/facebook
 /logo_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also available as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jp
 g) — brand-mark anchor post\, matches §8's original pick of logo.jpg.\n
 \nAlt text: "SaveTheHives logo."\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as-is — 
 no preview card will generate since a photo is attached.\n\nSuggested timi
 ng: Sun Aug 16\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §8.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
